<section class="sec" id="wrong">
  <p class="marginal"><span class="num">Section 07</span></p>
  <h2 class="t2">Four things I had to take back</h2>
  <p class="sub">This is the part of the log I would keep if I had to delete the rest.</p>
</section>
<div class="note corr">
  <span>Correction, the Flatpak that was never slow</span>
  <p>The benchmark measured the Flatpak at 731 ms, seven runs, a 15 ms spread. I published it as the worst number in the matrix. Run on its own, with nothing rebuilt, it takes 282 ms.</p>
  <p>The cause was my own rule about interleaving. The harness alternated two branches of the same Flatpak, same application id, therefore one shared QML cache directory, where compiled units are keyed by source path and modification time. Each launch invalidated the other's entries and every single run recompiled all 86 files. Wipe the cache, launch three times, and you get 560, 133, 141. Alternate the branches again and 743 ms comes back on demand. Interleaving protects you from drift between sessions, and it will happily invent a result when the two arms share state.</p>
  <p>The artefact was pointing at a real bug, which is the only reason that fake number was worth anything: it is what made me look at when the QML cache is cold, and that is the update cliff in section 03.</p>
</div>
<div class="note corr">
  <span>Correction, 1,716 lines that weighed nothing</span>
  <p>The main QML file is 4,276 lines, of which 1,716 are inline component blocks for dialogs that start inactive. Obviously that weight is loaded on every launch. Obviously moving them into their own files would shrink the largest compilation unit.</p>
  <p>I stripped all 1,716 lines and re-measured: 33 ms against 34 ms. File size is not the cost. Loading the types the file actually uses is the cost. A large, risky refactor got priced at zero before anyone wrote a line of it, which is the cheapest outcome available.</p>
</div>
<div class="prose">
  <p class="marginal corr"><span>Correction, a faster answer that was wrong</span>Resolving a file's type by unique filename glob instead of sniffing its contents was about eight times faster. It also disagreed with the correct answer on 2,054 real files on my disk: XHTML files named <code>.html</code>, TypeScript modules named <code>.mts</code>. Not committed.</p>
  <p>Being fast is not a defence. A file type resolver that is wrong twice in two thousand files is wrong in the preview pane, in the icon, and in the menu that decides what you are allowed to do with the file.</p>
</div>
<div class="note corr">
  <span>Correction, a leak that did not exist</span>
  <p>The interaction harness appeared to leak about 7 MB per navigation, 40 MB per loop. I wrote an idle memory reclaimer to fix it. The harness was pumping the event loop in a way that never runs deferred deletions, so the "leak" was objects queued for deletion that nothing was deleting. One line in the harness fixed it, the reclaimer went in the bin, and the real application running a real event loop never had the problem at all.</p>
</div>
<div class="prose">
  <p>Two smaller ones, for completeness. I recorded that ahead-of-time QML compilation was not worth it, which was true for the case I measured, a native build with a warm cache, and wrong as a general statement, since the warm cache only exists after a first launch. And I spent a while blaming a test failure on parallelism when it was failing because I had exported an environment variable that made Qt draw to nowhere, while the test counted real windows.</p>
</div>
<div class="pull">Every wrong number in that list came from the same habit: I measured once, got a plausible answer, and stopped. The correct number takes ten runs and four more minutes.</div>

<section class="sec" id="traps">
  <p class="marginal"><span class="num">Section 08</span></p>
  <h2 class="t2">Traps, and what they cost</h2>
  <p class="sub">Each of these cost at least an hour. They are in no particular order because pain has no ordering.</p>
</section>
<div class="prose">
  <ul>
    <li>A profiler changes what it measures. Qt's QML profiler forces recompilation even when compiled units are linked into the binary, so the compilation rows in its output are fabricated. I believed them for a while.</li>
    <li>A busy-wait in the benchmark drowns the signal. Whole-session CPU came out at 53 to 55 s for every renderer except one, because the settle loop was pegging a core and reporting it as the app's.</li>
    <li><code>pkill -f pattern</code> kills the calling shell when the pattern appears in the shell's own command line, including inside a heredoc. I did this to myself five times before moving every kill pattern into a script file. Five.</li>
    <li>HyprFM rewrites its session file on quit, so a killed instance can save after a script has restored the user's file. This destroyed a real session of mine once, which is why every benchmark launch now runs against a sandboxed home.</li>
    <li>If your own copy of the app is already running, a benchmark launch is forwarded to it over the single-instance socket and no new window appears. The harness waits patiently for a window that already exists.</li>
    <li>A scratch file called <code>bisect.py</code> shadows Python's standard library <code>bisect</code>, which breaks <code>statistics</code>, which is how the harness computes medians. Half an hour, and it was not even a real bug.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

